{"name":"Snorkel Blue","hex":"#034f84","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"snorkel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hollow tube, held in the mouth, or mounted on and opening into a diving mask, used by swimmers for breathing underwater.","A retractable tube fitted in diesel-engine submarines to allow sufficient ventilation that the engines may be used at periscope depth."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To use a snorkel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snorkel"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Snorkel (swimming)","blocks":[{"text":"A snorkel is a device used for breathing atmospheric air when the wearer's head is face downward in the surface water with the mouth and the nose submerged. It may be either a separate unit, or integrated into a swimming or diving mask. The integrated version is only suitable for surface snorkeling, while the separate device may also be used for surface breathing during breathhold underwater activities such as spearfishing, freediving, finswimming, underwater hockey, underwater rugby and for surface breathing while wearing scuba equipment. A standard snorkel is a curved tube with a shape usually resembling the letter \"L\" or \"J\", fitted with a mouthpiece at the lower end and made from plastic, synthetic elastomers, rubber, or light metal. The snorkel may have a loop or a clip to attach it to the head strap of the diving mask or swimming goggles, or may be tucked between the mask-strap and the head, or may be provided with its own head strap. Some snorkels are fitted with a float valve at the top to prevent flooding if the top opening is immersed, and some are fitted with a water trap and purge valve, intended for draining water from the tube."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Vieques_underwater_a.jpg/330px-Vieques_underwater_a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snorkel_(swimming)"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Snorkel.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Snorkel.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Side-mounted underwater swimmer's snorkel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASnorkel.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Britmarine_1970s_Catalogue_Snorkel_Page.jpg/500px-Britmarine_1970s_Catalogue_Snorkel_Page.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Britmarine_1970s_Catalogue_Snorkel_Page.jpg/500px-Britmarine_1970s_Catalogue_Snorkel_Page.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A range of 1970s snorkels made to British Standard BS 4532","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABritmarine_1970s_Catalogue_Snorkel_Page.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Reef_Snorkelling_on_the_Great_Barrier_Reef.jpg/500px-Reef_Snorkelling_on_the_Great_Barrier_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Reef_Snorkelling_on_the_Great_Barrier_Reef.jpg/1280px-Reef_Snorkelling_on_the_Great_Barrier_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Reef_Snorkelling_on_the_Great_Barrier_Reef.jpg/500px-Reef_Snorkelling_on_the_Great_Barrier_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Reef Snorkelling on the Great Barrier Reef","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReef_Snorkelling_on_the_Great_Barrier_Reef.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Tuba-nap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Tuba-nap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Front-mounted competitive swimmer's snorkel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATuba-nap.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Mittelschnorchel.jpg/500px-Mittelschnorchel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Mittelschnorchel.jpg/1280px-Mittelschnorchel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi